---
const rows = [
  { part: 'Raspberry Pi 4 (4GB)', price: '$55' },
  { part: 'USB webcam', price: '$30' },
  { part: 'Microphone (ReSpeaker Lite recommended)', price: '$25' },
  { part: 'Small HDMI panel', price: '$50' },
  { part: 'Powered speakers', price: '$20' },
];
const wiki = 'https://github.com/zlatko-lakisic/comstar/wiki';
---
<section class="section" id="build">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <h2>About $200 and a weekend</h2>
    <ul class="bom-list">
      {rows.map((r) => (
        <li>
          <span>{r.part}</span>
          <span class="bom-price">{r.price}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="bom-total">Terminal total <strong>$180</strong></p>
    <p class="bom-plus">Plus: a home server with an NVIDIA GPU.</p>
    <div class="caveat">
      The GPU box is the real requirement. COMSTAR is a thin terminal: the Pi
      handles capture and display, and a machine with an NVIDIA card does the
      thinking, seeing and speaking. If you already run a home server with a
      GPU for Frigate, Ollama, or Plex transcoding, you have it. If you don’t,
      that’s the cost of the project.
    </div>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${wiki}/Getting-Started`}>Getting started</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${wiki}/Hardware`}>Full parts list</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${wiki}/Architecture`}>Architecture</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
